-- js-dataflow.rofl — ONE cell, modelled: variable-to-variable value flow
-- through a declarator initialiser. Written over the scanner's four relations
-- plus rules/js-structure.rofl (which is where `ast_name` now comes from —
-- LOAD IT FIRST, or every rule here is silent for want of a premise). The
-- scanner judged nothing and this file does the judging.
--
-- DELIBERATELY NARROW. It sees `let x = y` and nothing more: not `x = y`
-- (assignment expression), not `let x = y + 1` (the init is not an identifier),
-- not a flow through a call or a property. Those are separate cells and the
-- audit is what remembers they are missing.

-- an identifier node, with its name.
-- ARITY NOTE: ast_node carries File since the scanner became complete, and
-- ast_child carries Index. Both extra columns are `_` here because this cell
-- is about names and not about position — but the Index column is why the
-- scanner can now tell `f(a, b)` from `f(b, a)`, which this cell does not
-- need and the next one will.
ident[code](N, Name)        :- ast_node[code](N, identifier, _, _), ast_name[code](N, Name).
-- ...AND THE SAME JOIN CARRYING ITS FILE, added 2026-09-05 for a reason that is
-- entirely about COST and not at all about meaning. Eight rules below used to
-- spell `ast_node[code](E, identifier, File, _), ast_name[code](E, Name)` as
-- their FIRST two literals, which enumerates every identifier in the corpus and
-- only then filters by name. Measured: that one premise handed out 876 391 of
-- the fixpoint's 2 246 605 rows — 39% of the whole evaluation — because the
-- engine reads a body left to right and has no join planner. Written as one
-- relation the store can index on (Name, File), the same eight rules ask a
-- selective question instead of a scan.
ident_in[code](N, Name, File) :- ast_node[code](N, identifier, File, _), ast_name[code](N, Name).

-- a declarator binds Name (its `id`) and reads Src (its `init`), both identifiers
decl_binds[code](D, Name)   :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, _, _),
                               ast_child[code](D, id, _, N), ident[code](N, Name).
decl_reads[code](D, Name)   :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, _, _),
                               ast_child[code](D, init, _, N), ident[code](N, Name).

-- THE MODELLED CLAIM: a value flows from the name read to the name bound.
var_flow[code](From, To)    :- decl_reads[code](D, From), decl_binds[code](D, To).

-- and its transitive closure, because a chain is what a dataflow trace is
var_reaches[code](A, B)     :- var_flow[code](A, B).
var_reaches[code](A, C)     :- var_reaches[code](A, B), var_flow[code](B, C).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE VALUE CORE — 2026-09-04, queue item w_df_value_core
--
-- Everything above moves values between NAMES and never says what a value IS.
-- That is why the layer had three ticks and thirty-five open cells: three ways
-- to move a thing, and no notion of the thing.
--
-- WHAT A VALUE IS HERE, and the choice is the whole design:
--
--   may_be_lit[flow](E, Text)  — E may evaluate to this literal, as written
--   may_be_node[flow](E, N)    — E may evaluate to the object, function or
--                                class AT NODE N — the thing itself, by identity
--
-- TWO CARRIERS AND NO THIRD, and in particular NO `unknown`. In a relational
-- language the absence of a row already means `nothing here says so`, and
-- adding a value to spell it would force every rule downstream to handle a
-- case the engine handles by not firing. This is the same argument that keeps
-- `not_modelled` a DERIVED default in the coverage model rather than a fact
-- somebody writes.
--
-- IT IS A MAY-SET AND THE NAME SAYS SO. Two rows mean `either of these`, never
-- `exactly these two`: a value the rules cannot see leaves no row, so the set
-- is an UNDER-approximation and a reader may add an edge on it but may never
-- conclude an absence from it. That is exactly the direction the call graph
-- needs — a missing row costs an edge, which the execution oracle measures as
-- an under-report — and exactly the direction a `this is definitely not X`
-- rule must never be written in.
--
-- THE DOMAIN IS FINITE BY CONSTRUCTION, which is what makes this safe to put
-- in rules at all: a literal's text and a node's identity both come FROM the
-- parsed program, so no rule here can manufacture a value the tree does not
-- already contain. Nothing widens, nothing needs a join, and the fixpoint
-- terminates for the same reason the string destructors do.

-- ENTRY 1 — a literal is its own value. `ast_value` is the scanner's `value`
-- attribute under the name rules/js-structure.rofl gives it.
edb(literal_kind).
literal_kind(string_literal).
literal_kind(numeric_literal).
literal_kind(boolean_literal).
-- A BIGINT IS A LITERAL LIKE ANY OTHER AND ITS VALUE WAS NOT ON THE STORE,
-- 2026-09-08 (w_update_and_literals). This row is one word and the measurement
-- under it is the item: `BigIntLiteral.value` is a native `bigint`, which
-- matched none of the four branches in scanners/js_ast.ts, so `const b = 1n`
-- emitted one `ast_node` fact and NO `ast_attr` — the only literal in the
-- language whose value the scanner dropped, silently, while its own contract
-- line said `every scalar own property`. Adding the row without the scanner
-- fix would have derived nothing at all, which is why the fix came first and
-- the mutant that removes it kills this arm rather than the row.
--
-- ITS TERM IS THE DECIMAL STRING and the collision is stated: `1n` and the
-- string `"1"` are the same term here, exactly as `1e21` and its decimal
-- spelling already are. See scanners/js_ast.ts for why the alternative — a
-- bare integer, colliding with the NUMBER `1` — is the worse of the two.
literal_kind(big_int_literal).

may_be_lit[flow](E, V) :- ast_node[code](E, K, _, _), literal_kind(K), ast_value[code](E, V).

-- A TEMPLATE WITH NO INTERPOLATION IS A STRING WRITTEN THE OTHER WAY, and
-- until 2026-09-08 this model could not say so. The text lives in
-- `TemplateElement.value`, a nested object, and scanners/js_ast.ts emitted
-- scalar own properties only — a sentence that blocked FOUR cells under the
-- cause `scanner_contract` with the note `it moves when the scanner`s contract
-- moves`. Measured before moving it: over 223 files and 422 482 nodes, with
-- babel`s `extra` scratch declared out, that contract excluded exactly ONE
-- property in the whole language. So the contract moved, and this is a join
-- rather than a mechanism.
--
-- `cooked` AND NOT `raw`. `raw` is the source text with its escapes still in
-- it; what a template EVALUATES to is the cooked form. They differ exactly
-- where an escape appears, which is the only place the choice is observable —
-- and a key written `` obj[`a\tb`] `` selects a tab, not a backslash and a t.
--
-- NO ARM FOR AN INTERPOLATED TEMPLATE, deliberately: `` `a${n}b` `` evaluates
-- to a string this layer cannot name, and `may_be_lit` carrying a partial one
-- would be a wrong answer where silence is the right one. Same reasoning as
-- `plain_assign` refusing a compound assignment because it evaluates to a sum.
interpolated[code](T)  :- ast_child[code](T, expressions, _, _).
may_be_lit[flow](T, V) :- ast_node[code](T, template_literal, _, _),
                          not interpolated[code](T),
                          ast_child[code](T, quasis, 0, Q),
                          ast_attr[code](Q, value_cooked, V).

-- ENTRY 2 — an object, function or class expression is its own node. The
-- expression IS the thing; there is nothing to look up.
edb(node_value_kind).
node_value_kind(object_expression).
node_value_kind(array_expression).
node_value_kind(function_expression).
node_value_kind(arrow_function_expression).
node_value_kind(class_declaration).
node_value_kind(class_expression).
-- THE ONE LITERAL IN JAVASCRIPT WHOSE VALUE IS AN OBJECT, 2026-09-08
-- (w_update_and_literals). `/ab+c/gi` is written like `1n` and behaves like
-- `{}`: it has methods, it has identity, and `/x/ === /x/` is false. So it
-- belongs to THIS list and not to `literal_kind` — a `may_be_lit` row would
-- have to name a text, and the text of a regexp is not what the expression
-- evaluates to. The scanner gives it `pattern` and `flags` and NO `value`
-- attribute, so the `literal_kind` arm could not have reached it anyway;
-- measured before the row was written, `may_be_node` and `prototype_of` were
-- both EMPTY for both receivers in the fixture.
--
-- SAME OVER-APPROXIMATION AS ITS NEIGHBOURS, stated because a regexp is where
-- it is easiest to notice: a literal inside a loop evaluates to a NEW object
-- each time round, so `may_be_node(E, E)` names the site rather than the
-- object — which is exactly what `object_expression` and `array_expression`
-- have always meant here.
node_value_kind(reg_exp_literal).

may_be_node[flow](E, E) :- ast_node[code](E, K, _, _), node_value_kind(K).

-- ENTRY 3 — a name may be whatever its binder's initialiser may be.
--
-- FILE-SCOPED UNTIL 2026-09-07, and the sentence that stood here said so: two
-- `const k` in two functions of one file were one name, so a name bound twice
-- carried both values, declared as honest over-approximation for a may-set.
--
-- IT HAD NO WITNESS, AND THE REASON IS THE FINDING. `binder` is bound twice in
-- this corpus — `const c = new Crate()` in `useCrate` and `const c = new
-- Cask(n)` in `useSuper` — and the collision derived nothing wrong for as long
-- as the two classes SHARED NO METHOD NAME. Five fixture renames over the
-- preceding week were made specifically to dodge this limitation, so the corpus
-- had been bent around the defect until the defect could not be seen. Giving
-- `Crate` a `hold` to match `Barrel`'s took `ambiguous_call[audit]` from 8 to
-- 12 in one method.
binder[code](D, Name, Init, File) :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](D, id, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name),
                                     ast_child[code](D, init, 0, Init).

-- A BINDER'S REGION is the function it sits in, or the FILE when it sits in
-- none. A use sees a binder when the binder's region contains the use — which
-- is what makes two `const c` in two functions two names again.
-- ONE NOTION OF SCOPE, AND MORE THAN ONE WAY TO BIND. `scoped_binder` exists so
-- that the three rules below name the thing they care about — a declarator that
-- introduces names in a region — rather than the one relation that happened to
-- be the only such thing. Added 2026-09-08 with destructuring, which binds
-- names from a PATTERN and therefore cannot be a `binder` row: `binder`'s third
-- argument is the node the name evaluates to, and a destructured name evaluates
-- to a MEMBER of that node rather than to the node.
--
-- THE REFACTOR SHIPPED BEFORE THE FEATURE AND WITH ITS OWN CONTROL: with only
-- this indirection in place and no second arm, fifteen relations of this layer
-- were compared row for row against the previous form and came back IDENTICAL.
scoped_binder[code](D, File) :- binder[code](D, _, _, File).

binder_region[code](D, R)    :- scoped_binder[code](D, _), nearest_v[flow](R, D).
binder_at_top[code](D)       :- scoped_binder[code](D, _), not binder_region[code](D, _).
--
-- `ident_in[code](E, _, _)` IS A COST LITERAL AND NOTHING ELSE, and it is here
-- because the first version of this paragraph nearly doubled the fixpoint. A
-- USE is an identifier; the two consumers below both join `sees_binder` against
-- `ident_in`, so ranging E over every node under the region materialised a
-- (node x binder) product that no rule could ever read. Measured as a 2x2 over
-- {HEAD rules, these rules} x {HEAD corpus, this corpus}, so the rules and the
-- fixture are separated rather than argued about:
--
--     wide E: rules alone   facts +51.8%  firings +71.8%
--     narrow: rules alone   facts +18.2%  firings +25.0%
--
-- Fifteen relations compared row-for-row between the two forms and IDENTICAL,
-- with a positive control (removing `object_method` from `this_binds_kind`
-- moves seven of them). The cheaper form is the same model.
sees_binder[code](E, D)      :- binder_region[code](D, R), ast_within[code](R, E),
                                ident_in[code](E, _, _).
-- ...and a top-level binder is visible everywhere in its own file, which is the
-- honest reading: `const seeded = run(1)` at module scope really is in scope
-- inside every function below it.
sees_binder[code](E, D)      :- binder_at_top[code](D), scoped_binder[code](D, File),
                                ident_in[code](E, _, File).

-- WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated rather than discovered later: SHADOWING. Two
-- binders of one name where one region contains the other are both visible at a
-- use inside the inner one, and nothing here prefers the nearer. That needs
-- block regions and a nearest-wins rule, it needs its own fixture, and it is
-- w_scope_shadowing. The direction is the safe one — an extra candidate in a
-- may-set — and it is the same shape `class_receiver` declares one file over.
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- binder[code](D, Name, Init, File), may_be_lit[flow](Init, V),
                           ident_in[code](E, Name, File), sees_binder[code](E, D).
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- binder[code](D, Name, Init, File), may_be_node[flow](Init, N),
                           ident_in[code](E, Name, File), sees_binder[code](E, D).

-- ENTRY 4 — assignment. `x = "a"` makes `x` may-be "a" WHEREVER x appears in
-- the file, which is the flow-insensitive reading and is stated rather than
-- hidden: this layer has no notion of before and after, so it cannot say that
-- the value at line 10 is not the value at line 3. A flow-SENSITIVE reading
-- needs the control-flow layer, which does not exist yet, and that dependency
-- is the reason `w_controlflow_layer` sits in the queue at all.
assigns[code](Name, Src, File) :- ast_node[code](A, assignment_expression, File, _),
                                  ast_child[code](A, left, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Name),
                                  ast_child[code](A, right, 0, Src).

may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- assigns[code](Name, Src, File), may_be_lit[flow](Src, V),
                           ident_in[code](E, Name, File).
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- assigns[code](Name, Src, File), may_be_node[flow](Src, N),
                           ident_in[code](E, Name, File).

-- ENTRY 5 — the transparent wrappers. A parenthesis, a TS cast and a non-null
-- assertion change nothing about the value, which is exactly why the call
-- graph lists them as separate SHAPES and this layer does not: they are one
-- rule over a table of kinds.
edb(value_transparent).
value_transparent(parenthesized_expression).
value_transparent(tsas_expression).
value_transparent(tsnon_null_expression).

may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- value_transparent(K), ast_node[code](E, K, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, expression, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- value_transparent(K), ast_node[code](E, K, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, expression, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).

-- WHAT THE LAYER CAN SAY ABOUT ITSELF: an expression the rules can value at
-- all. A site with no row is not `no value` — it is `nothing here says so`.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHICH PROTOTYPE A VALUE HAS (w_prototype_of_a_value, 49).
--
-- THE ITEM SAID THERE WAS NO SITE AND THE MODEL SAYS THERE ARE TWELVE. Its note
-- read `NO SITE IN THE CORPUS, measured 2026-09-07: 13 member calls unresolved,
-- zero with a literal receiver`, and that measurement asked `may_be_lit` — does
-- the model carry this receiver as a VALUE. A prototype is not a value question.
-- `[1, 2, 3].join(",")` needs nothing carried at all: the receiver is an
-- `array_expression` and the KIND is the answer. Re-measured 2026-09-08, every
-- unresolved member call with its receiver named:
--
--   template_literal .concat   1     the String prototype
--   array_expression .join     1     the Array prototype
--   identifier       .next     6     a generator object, and `may_be_node` is
--                                    EMPTY for all six - the value half of the
--                                    generator protocol, not this question
--   new_expression / identifier 2    a class IN this program; not a prototype
--                                    question at all
--
-- So the site count for this relation is TWO, the six `.next` calls belong to a
-- different item, and `no site in the corpus` was true of the question it was
-- asked and false of the question the item names.
--
-- FROM THE KIND, AND SEPARATELY THROUGH THE VALUE LAYER. The first arm answers
-- a receiver written in place; the second answers one reached through a binder,
-- an assignment or a return, and it costs nothing because `may_be_node` is
-- already standing. A template literal is in NEITHER `literal_kind` nor
-- `node_value_kind`, so only the first arm reaches it — which is why the arm on
-- the kind exists rather than being folded into the value one.
edb(kind_prototype).   -- kind_prototype(Kind, Prototype)
kind_prototype(array_expression,          array).
kind_prototype(string_literal,            string).
kind_prototype(template_literal,          string).
kind_prototype(numeric_literal,           number).
kind_prototype(boolean_literal,           boolean).
kind_prototype(object_expression,         object).
kind_prototype(function_expression,       function).
kind_prototype(arrow_function_expression, function).
-- TWO MORE, 2026-09-08 (w_update_and_literals), and both were measured as
-- SILENT before they were written rather than assumed to be missing.
-- `/x[0-9]/.test(s)` and `255n.toString(16)` are member calls on a literal;
-- both were `unresolved_call` with `prototype_of` EMPTY, so `stdlib_member`
-- could not name them and the residue was invisible at the one place the model
-- collects it. A regexp goes through both arms — the inline receiver by KIND,
-- `MATCHER.test(s)` through `may_be_node` — which is why the fixture writes
-- it twice.
kind_prototype(reg_exp_literal,           regexp).
kind_prototype(big_int_literal,           bigint).

prototype_of[flow](E, P) :- kind_prototype(K, P), ast_node[code](E, K, _, _).
prototype_of[flow](E, P) :- may_be_node[flow](E, N), kind_prototype(K, P),
                            ast_node[code](N, K, _, _).

-- WHOSE PROTOTYPE IS THE STANDARD LIBRARY'S. `object` and `function` are NOT on
-- this list and the omission is the point: a member on an object literal is
-- answered by `member_value`, and a member on a function is `.call`/`.bind`,
-- which is a question about the language rather than a gap in this model. The
-- four that remain are the ones whose methods live in a library this model does
-- not have — which is what `w_env_api_surface` is for, and it waits on this
-- relation by name.
edb(builtin_prototype).
builtin_prototype(array).
builtin_prototype(string).
builtin_prototype(number).
builtin_prototype(boolean).
-- ...AND TWO WHOSE METHODS ARE THE WHOLE POINT OF THE FORM, 2026-09-08. A
-- regexp literal is USED by calling `.test`/`.exec` on it, so unlike `object`
-- and `function` above there is no in-program answer to route to first: every
-- member of a regexp lives in the library. `bigint` is here for the same
-- reason `number` is. The atom `bigint` is also a `feature` in
-- facts/js-env.rofl; different relation, and the two never meet.
builtin_prototype(regexp).
builtin_prototype(bigint).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DESTRUCTURING: A NAME BOUND FROM A PATTERN RATHER THAN FROM A VALUE.
--
-- `const { pulled: taken } = drawer` binds `taken` to the MEMBER `pulled` of
-- whatever `drawer` is, and `binder[code]` cannot say that: its third argument
-- is the node the name evaluates to, and here the name evaluates to a member of
-- that node. A declarator whose `id` is an `object_pattern` has no `ast_name`
-- at all, so it produced no `binder` row and the names it introduces were
-- invisible to the whole value layer.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE ANY OF THIS: on a three-line probe with `const { pick } =
-- holder` beside a direct `holder.pick(n)`, the model derived the direct edge
-- and lost the destructured one entirely, and `vocabulary_gap[audit]` named
-- `object_pattern` the same run. So the hole was DECLARED rather than silent —
-- it was waiting for a fixture, which is exactly what that audit is for.
--
-- EVERY FACT THIS NEEDS WAS ALREADY THERE. `member_value[flow]` answers what a
-- key holds and `may_be_node` carries it, so the arm is a join: the local name
-- may be whatever the init`s member of that key may be. `key_name` rather than
-- `ast_name` on the key, for the reason four other readers of a key learned.
--
-- SHORTHAND AND RENAME ARE ONE CASE, not two. `{ pulled }` gives an
-- `object_property` whose key and value are two identifiers of the same name,
-- and `{ pulled: taken }` gives two of different names — so reading the key for
-- the member and the value for the local covers both without a `shorthand`
-- test, and the corpus`s fixture uses the rename because the shorthand form
-- would shadow the function it binds.
destructures[code](D, Local, Key, File) :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, File, _),
                                           ast_child[code](D, id, 0, P),
                                           ast_node[code](P, object_pattern, _, _),
                                           ast_child[code](P, properties, _, Prop),
                                           ast_child[code](Prop, key, 0, K), key_name[code](K, Key),
                                           ast_child[code](Prop, value, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Local).
scoped_binder[code](D, File) :- destructures[code](D, _, _, File).

may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- destructures[code](D, Local, Key, File),
                           ast_child[code](D, init, 0, Init), may_be_node[flow](Init, Obj),
                           member_value[flow](Obj, Key, V), may_be_node[flow](V, N),
                           ident_in[code](E, Local, File), sees_binder[code](E, D).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE OTHER FOUR FORMS OF THE SAME FAMILY (w_destructuring_rest_and_spread).
--
-- `object_pattern` landed one iteration ago and `array_pattern`,
-- `assignment_pattern`, `rest_element` and `spread_element` had no site in the
-- corpus at all. MEASURED WITH THE FIXTURES IN AND NO RULE WRITTEN: the value
-- layer answered NOTHING at seven of the eight new sites, and answered the
-- eighth WRONGLY — see the spread-argument paragraph below, which is the one
-- place this family made the model say something false rather than nothing.

-- AN ARRAY PATTERN BINDS BY POSITION, which is the whole difference from an
-- object pattern: `{ a }` names its member and `[a, b]` counts to it. The
-- INDEX the scanner carries is therefore the content of the relation, exactly
-- as it is for an argument — and it is counted over the RAW array, so a hole
-- in `[, second]` leaves `second` at index 1 rather than sliding it to 0.
destructures_at[code](D, Local, Index, File) :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, File, _),
                                                ast_child[code](D, id, 0, P),
                                                ast_node[code](P, array_pattern, _, _),
                                                ast_child[code](P, elements, Index, L),
                                                ast_name[code](L, Local).
scoped_binder[code](D, File) :- destructures_at[code](D, _, _, File).

-- WHAT AN ARRAY HOLDS AT A POSITION. `iter_elem[flow]` further down answers
-- the same question for `for-of` and deliberately DROPS the index, because a
-- loop variable takes every element in turn. Two readers, two questions, and
-- the index is the difference: this one is also what expands a spread argument.
elem_at[flow](X, I, E) :- may_be_node[flow](X, A), ast_node[code](A, array_expression, _, _),
                          ast_child[code](A, elements, I, E).

may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- destructures_at[code](D, Local, Index, File),
                           ast_child[code](D, init, 0, Init), elem_at[flow](Init, Index, V),
                           may_be_node[flow](V, N),
                           ident_in[code](E, Local, File), sees_binder[code](E, D).

-- NO `may_be_lit` ARM HERE, and it is left out on purpose rather than
-- forgotten: nothing in this corpus destructures a literal out of an array, so
-- the arm would be a clause no mutant could kill. The rule this repository
-- already paid for — an arm nothing exercises cannot go red — applies to a
-- symmetry as much as to a feature.

-- A DEFAULTED PARAMETER WAS INVISIBLE TWICE OVER. `param_of[flow]` reads
-- `ast_name` off the `params` child and an `assignment_pattern` has none, so a
-- defaulted parameter had NO INDEX: neither did the default reach the body,
-- nor did an argument PASSED at that position. Both halves are below and they
-- are separate arms because they are separate claims — measured apart with two
-- call sites, one omitting the argument and one supplying it.
param_of[flow](F, I, Name) :- fn_node_v[flow](F), ast_child[code](F, params, I, P),
                              ast_node[code](P, assignment_pattern, _, _),
                              ast_child[code](P, left, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Name).

param_default[flow](F, Name, Init) :- fn_node_v[flow](F), ast_child[code](F, params, _, P),
                                      ast_node[code](P, assignment_pattern, _, _),
                                      ast_child[code](P, left, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Name),
                                      ast_child[code](P, right, 0, Init).

may_be_node[flow](U, N) :- param_default[flow](F, Name, Init), may_be_node[flow](Init, N),
                           param_use[flow](F, Name, U).

-- AN OBJECT REST BINDS A FRESH OBJECT and that object has no node of its own
-- in the source — so the model gives it the `rest_element` node's identity.
-- The rest node IS the object, in the same sense that an `object_expression`
-- is the object it writes: both are values named by a node the store already
-- has, which is the whole point of `may_be_node` carrying identity.
pattern_takes[code](P, Key) :- ast_node[code](P, object_pattern, _, _),
                               ast_child[code](P, properties, _, Prop),
                               ast_child[code](Prop, key, 0, K), key_name[code](K, Key).

rest_in_pattern[code](D, R, File) :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](D, id, 0, P),
                                     ast_node[code](P, object_pattern, _, _),
                                     ast_child[code](P, properties, _, R),
                                     ast_node[code](R, rest_element, _, _).
rest_binds[code](D, R, Local, File) :- rest_in_pattern[code](D, R, File),
                                       ast_child[code](R, argument, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Local).
scoped_binder[code](D, File) :- rest_binds[code](D, _, _, File).

-- ...AND THE EXCLUSION IS THE WHOLE CONTENT OF THE RELATION. `{ a, ...rest }`
-- gives `rest` every key EXCEPT `a`, and a rule that copied them all would
-- answer `rest.a` — which is `undefined` and throws when called. No runnable
-- fixture can hold that site, so it lives in shapes.ts and it is the only
-- thing that tells the two rules apart.
member_value[flow](R, Key, V) :- rest_in_pattern[code](D, R, _),
                                 ast_child[code](D, id, 0, P),
                                 ast_child[code](D, init, 0, Init), may_be_node[flow](Init, Obj),
                                 member_value[flow](Obj, Key, V),
                                 not pattern_takes[code](P, Key).
-- a rest object is PLAIN in the sense `member_plain` means: no `static`
-- attribute anywhere on it, so neither class arm reaches it and the member
-- lookup would find nothing without this row. Same reason the module namespace
-- needed one, and measured the same way.
member_plain[flow](R, Key, V) :- rest_in_pattern[code](_, R, _), member_value[flow](R, Key, V).

may_be_node[flow](E, R) :- rest_binds[code](D, R, Local, File),
                           ident_in[code](E, Local, File), sees_binder[code](E, D).

-- AN OBJECT SPREAD COPIES THE KEYS OF WHAT IT SPREADS, so a member of the
-- result is a member of the source reached through one more node. This is an
-- ordinary `member_value` arm and it needs no new carrier at all.
--
-- OVER-APPROXIMATION DECLARED: `{ ...o, k: v }` overrides `o`'s own `k`, and
-- this rule keeps both. The direction is the safe one for a may-set — an extra
-- candidate, never a missing one — and narrowing it needs an order this layer
-- does not have, which is the same sentence `assigns` carries about `x = a`
-- then `x = b`.
member_value[flow](O, Key, V) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](O, properties, _, S),
                                 ast_node[code](S, spread_element, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](S, argument, 0, A), may_be_node[flow](A, Src),
                                 member_value[flow](Src, Key, V).

valued[flow](E) :- may_be_lit[flow](E, _).
valued[flow](E) :- may_be_node[flow](E, _).

-- ===========================================================================
-- ACROSS A CALL — 2026-09-04, queue item w_df_function_forms
--
-- THE HONEST STATEMENT FIRST, BECAUSE IT BREAKS THE LAYER PICTURE: to know what
-- an argument gives a parameter you must know WHICH FUNCTION the call reaches,
-- and to resolve a callee you must know what its expression MAY BE. The call
-- graph and the value flow are ONE FIXPOINT, not two floors stacked. So the
-- rules below read `resolves[code]` from rules/js-callgraph.rofl while that
-- file reads `may_be_lit[flow]` from here, and neither can be computed first.
-- The kernel takes it for the same reason it took tier 3's smaller cycle: the
-- recursion is positive and the negations both packs stand on sit outside it.
--
-- Loaded alone this half is simply silent — nothing concludes `resolves`, so
-- nothing fires — which is the correct behaviour for a pack whose partner is
-- absent, and is why test/js-ast.test.ts can still exercise the old cell.

edb(fn_kind_v).
fn_kind_v(function_declaration).
fn_kind_v(function_expression).
fn_kind_v(arrow_function_expression).
fn_kind_v(object_method).
fn_kind_v(class_method).
fn_kind_v(class_private_method).

edb(call_like_v).
call_like_v(call_expression).
call_like_v(optional_call_expression).
call_like_v(new_expression).

fn_node_v[flow](F) :- ast_node[code](F, K, _, _), fn_kind_v(K).

-- THE NEAREST ENCLOSING FUNCTION, over ANY node rather than over call sites.
-- DUPLICATION DECLARED: rules/js-callgraph.rofl computes the same thing under
-- `nearest_fn`, restricted to sites. Two computations of one relation is a
-- smell and consolidating them is a separate change, because the call graph's
-- copy is the target of a mutant that would lose its anchor.
encloses_v[flow](F, X) :- fn_node_v[flow](F), ast_within[code](F, X).
closer_v[flow](F, X)   :- encloses_v[flow](F, X), encloses_v[flow](G, X),
                          ast_within[code](F, G), F != G.
nearest_v[flow](F, X)  :- encloses_v[flow](F, X), not closer_v[flow](F, X).

-- ARGUMENT INTO PARAMETER. The INDEX is the content — argument 0 and argument 1
-- are different facts about the program, which is why the scanner carries it.
-- A SPREAD ARGUMENT DESTROYS THE CORRESPONDENCE THIS RELATION EXISTS TO
-- CARRY, and the model said something FALSE about it before this was written.
-- MEASURED on `pair(...bench, cubed)` with `bench = [chiselled, planed]` and
-- the fixture running: the model derived `pair -> cubed`, because argument 1 in
-- the TREE is `cubed` while argument 1 at RUN TIME is `planed` — `bench`
-- contributes two arguments and everything after it shifts. The oracle says
-- `pair` calls `chiselled` and `planed` and nothing else.
--
-- A may-set is allowed to be SILENT and is not allowed to be WRONG, so the
-- positional arm stops at the first spread. Note this is not the spread node
-- itself being unvalued — that is silence and costs an edge; this is a
-- DIFFERENT argument getting an index that is not its own.
spread_arg[code](C, I)   :- ast_node[code](C, K, _, _), call_like_v(K),
                            ast_child[code](C, arguments, I, S),
                            ast_node[code](S, spread_element, _, _).
after_spread[code](C, I) :- spread_arg[code](C, J), ast_child[code](C, arguments, I, _), J < I.

arg_at[flow](C, I, A)      :- ast_node[code](C, K, _, _), call_like_v(K),
                              ast_child[code](C, arguments, I, A),
                              not ast_node[code](A, spread_element, _, _),
                              not after_spread[code](C, I).

-- ...AND THE CASE WHERE THE CORRESPONDENCE SURVIVES EXACTLY, which is not the
-- narrow one it first looks like. A spread at argument position J contributes
-- its element I at argument J + I — the arguments BEFORE it are unshifted by
-- definition, and the ones after it are already silenced above. So a spread of
-- an array this layer can see is answerable wherever it sits, and `elem_at` is
-- already the relation that says which element is where.
--
-- THE GUARD IS ON THE SPREAD ITSELF, not on what follows it: a spread that is
-- after another spread has been shifted by a count this layer does not carry.
-- DECLARED UNEXERCISED — `f(...a, ...b)` is in no fixture here, so no mutant of
-- this corpus can kill that literal, and the site that would is a call with two
-- spreads whose first array has a length other than one.
arg_at[flow](C, I, E)      :- spread_arg[code](C, J), not after_spread[code](C, J),
                              ast_child[code](C, arguments, J, S),
                              ast_child[code](S, argument, 0, A), elem_at[flow](A, K, E),
                              I is J + K.
param_of[flow](F, I, Name) :- fn_node_v[flow](F), ast_child[code](F, params, I, P),
                              ast_name[code](P, Name).

-- WHERE A PARAMETER IS READ. Over-approximation declared: any identifier of
-- that name anywhere under F, so a nested function redeclaring the name is
-- claimed here and should not be. Shadowing is the scope layer's cell.
-- ORDERED FOR COST, and the order is the whole rule: `param_of` binds F and
-- Name, `ast_within` with F bound hands back what is under that function, and
-- `ident` is then a two-bound probe. The other way round it asked for every
-- identifier of that name in the corpus first — measured at 168 rows a call
-- for a parameter named `n`, 1110 times.
param_use[flow](F, Name, U) :- param_of[flow](F, _, Name),
                               ast_within[code](F, U),
                               ident[code](U, Name).

may_be_lit[flow](U, V)  :- resolves[code](C, F), arg_at[flow](C, I, A), may_be_lit[flow](A, V),
                           param_of[flow](F, I, Name), param_use[flow](F, Name, U).
may_be_node[flow](U, N) :- resolves[code](C, F), arg_at[flow](C, I, A), may_be_node[flow](A, N),
                           param_of[flow](F, I, Name), param_use[flow](F, Name, U).

-- WHAT A CALL EVALUATES TO. `return E` inside F makes F's value E's value, and
-- a call that resolves to F may be whatever F returns. This is the rule
-- `f().g()` waits on: the call is not a name, so nothing about it could be
-- looked up until the layer could say what it EVALUATES to.
returns[flow](F, E) :- ast_node[code](R, return_statement, _, _), nearest_v[flow](F, R),
                       ast_child[code](R, argument, 0, E).

may_be_lit[flow](C, V)  :- resolves[code](C, F), returns[flow](F, E), may_be_lit[flow](E, V).
may_be_node[flow](C, N) :- resolves[code](C, F), returns[flow](F, E), may_be_node[flow](E, N).

-- ===========================================================================
-- READING A PROPERTY OFF A VALUE — the rest of w_df_value_core
--
-- This is the question the call graph has been answering privately. `denotes`
-- there carries an object-literal lookup, a class lookup and a `this` rule, and
-- every one of them is a DATAFLOW fact that happened to be written where it was
-- first needed — the same way `param_bind` was. They come home here, and the
-- call graph keeps only the projection it actually asks for.
--
-- WHAT AN OBJECT IS, for a value question: a node that can answer `.k`. An
-- object literal and a class are not alike in the language and are identical to
-- this question, which is the whole reason one relation covers both.
obj_like[flow](O) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _).
obj_like[flow](O) :- ast_node[code](O, class_declaration, _, _).
obj_like[flow](O) :- ast_node[code](O, class_expression, _, _).

-- MEMBER VALUE, over BOTH — a shorthand method, a property holding anything,
-- and a class method. Note what is NOT here: any judgement about what the value
-- IS. `member_value` says the member exists and names the node it holds; asking
-- what that node may be is `may_be_*`'s job, one step later.
member_value[flow](O, Key, V) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](O, properties, _, M),
                                 ast_node[code](M, object_method, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](M, key, 0, K), key_name[code](K, Key), V = M.
member_value[flow](O, Key, V) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](O, properties, _, P),
                                 ast_node[code](P, object_property, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](P, key, 0, K), key_name[code](K, Key),
                                 ast_child[code](P, value, 0, V).
member_value[flow](CD, Key, M) :- obj_like[flow](CD), ast_child[code](CD, body, 0, B),
                                  ast_child[code](B, body, _, M),
                                  ast_node[code](M, class_method, _, _),
                                  ast_child[code](M, key, 0, K), key_name[code](K, Key).

-- A MEMBER WRITTEN IS A MEMBER READ — `obj.x = f`, and then `obj.x()`.
--
-- WHAT THE QUEUE ITEM SAID, and it is the sixth design note in this loop to
-- lose to one probe: "there is no store, so a property written is not a
-- property read". THE STORE WAS NEVER THE MISSING THING. Entry 4 above already
-- reads an assignment to an IDENTIFIER flow-insensitively — `x = "a"` makes `x`
-- may-be "a" wherever x appears in the file — and this is that same reading one
-- step over. It needs no notion of before and after for exactly the same
-- reason, and it inherits the same declared imprecision: a property written
-- late is answered early, and every write to the same key is an alternative
-- rather than a replacement. That is a MAY-set, which is what this layer is.
--
-- MEASURED FIRST, and every piece was already standing: `selects[flow]` fires
-- on the member on the LEFT (it ranges over `member_node_v`, not over callee
-- positions), `may_be_node` answers the object half — including a `this`, which
-- is why `this.v = v` lands here too — and the right-hand side is already
-- valued. The arm joins them and adds no mechanism.
--
-- `plain_assign` IS LOAD-BEARING: `rack.tally += 5` evaluates to a SUM, and
-- this layer knows nothing about sums, so the compound forms contribute no
-- value rather than the wrong one.
--
-- ORDERED FOR COST: `plain_assign` is the smallest relation in the body and it
-- binds A, so nothing here enumerates the member nodes of the corpus.
-- AND ONE LITERAL WAS MEASURED DEAD BEFORE IT SHIPPED. The first draft led with
-- `member_node_v[flow](L)` to say "the left is a member expression"; deleting it
-- moved not one row, because `selects[flow]` is ITSELF defined over
-- `member_node_v` and says the same thing while binding Key. Written from a
-- model of what the rule needs rather than from the relations that exist —
-- the same shape as the `super` arm deleted five items ago, caught the same way,
-- by planting a delete-mutant on every literal rather than only on the arm.
member_value[flow](O, Key, V) :- plain_assign[flow](A), ast_child[code](A, left, 0, L),
                                 selects[flow](L, Key),
                                 ast_child[code](L, object, 0, Obj),
                                 may_be_node[flow](Obj, O),
                                 ast_child[code](A, right, 0, V).

-- AND WHAT THE CLASS INHERITS. A method declared on an ancestor is reachable on
-- an instance of the subclass, and the rule above sees only a class's OWN body,
-- so `new Cask(n).hold(n)` — `hold` declared two levels up — resolved to
-- nothing. FOUND BY ADDING THE `super()` FIXTURE rather than by reading: the
-- inheritance chain that exercise needed made the gap produce a site, and the
-- site made it a row. Before that the corpus had no class extending another
-- with a method to inherit, so the hole was invisible to every count.
--
-- `not own_key[flow]` is SHADOWING and it is what makes this a lookup rather
-- than a union: a subclass that declares `hold` answers with its own, and the
-- ancestor's is not also an answer. The negation is stratified — `own_key`
-- reads the class body directly and does not depend on `member_value`.
own_key[flow](CD, Key)         :- obj_like[flow](CD), ast_child[code](CD, body, 0, B),
                                  ast_child[code](B, body, _, M),
                                  ast_node[code](M, class_method, _, _),
                                  ast_child[code](M, key, 0, K), ast_name[code](K, Key).
--
-- THE NEGATION GOES LAST, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE. Written with `not own_key`
-- BEFORE the literal that binds `Key`, this rule silently under-derives: the
-- chain Cask -> Keg -> Barrel stops one level short, because `Cask` declares a
-- `constructor` and the negation, evaluated with `Key` still unbound, reads as
-- "Cask has no own key AT ALL". Isolated to ten lines: the same rule with the
-- two literals swapped answers `b c k` one way and `b k` the other, over
-- identical facts. `whynot` reports NO FAILING PREMISE for the missing row —
-- it binds the goal's arguments first, so it evaluates the negation with `Key`
-- bound and disagrees with the evaluator. Recorded as a kernel finding.
--
-- FIXED IN THE KERNEL 2026-09-05. `Evaluation.evalOrder` defers a negative
-- literal to the earliest point where every variable in it is bound, so the two
-- spellings above are one program again and mutant 22 in
-- test/js-callgraph.test.ts - which existed to STOP KILLING on this day - now
-- asserts they agree edge for edge. THE ORDER BELOW IS KEPT AS WRITTEN
-- ANYWAY: it is the order a reader follows, and after 2026-09-05 it is also the
-- order the cost work chose. What changed is that it is no longer LOAD-BEARING,
-- and a rule written the other way would answer the same.
member_value[flow](CD, Key, V) :- super_of[flow](CD, SD),
                                  member_value[flow](SD, Key, V),
                                  not own_key[flow](CD, Key).

-- THE KEY AN EXPRESSION SELECTS. Static and computed collapse here for the same
-- reason they collapsed in the call graph: `o.pick` and `o[k]` differ only in
-- where the text is, and `may_be_lit` is what closes the distance. A key nobody
-- can value reaches no row, which is the refusal arriving as an absence.
member_kind_v(member_expression).
member_kind_v(optional_member_expression).
edb(member_kind_v).

member_node_v[flow](N)      :- ast_node[code](N, K, _, _), member_kind_v(K).
selects[flow](N, Key)       :- member_node_v[flow](N), ast_attr[code](N, computed, false),
                               ast_child[code](N, property, 0, P), ast_name[code](P, Key).
selects[flow](N, Key)       :- member_node_v[flow](N), ast_attr[code](N, computed, true),
                               ast_child[code](N, property, 0, P), may_be_lit[flow](P, Key).

-- ENTRY 6 — a member expression may be whatever the member it selects may be.
-- RECURSIVE THROUGH may_be_node: `deep.layer.dig` is `.dig` in whatever
-- `deep.layer` may be, and `deep.layer` is `.layer` in whatever `deep` may be.
--
-- AND THE RECEIVER DECIDES WHICH HALF OF A CLASS IT CAN SEE, added 2026-09-05.
-- `Vat.poured()` and `new Vat().tapped()` are both TypeErrors at runtime and
-- both resolved here, because `new Vat()` and the name `Vat` reach the SAME
-- class node and `member_value` returns the whole body. The fixture that shows
-- it was written first and the two false edges appeared by name in the
-- over-approximation list — `useMethodOnClass -> poured` and
-- `useStaticOnInstance -> tapped` — which is the only reason this is a fix
-- rather than a guess.
--
-- THE FACT WAS ALWAYS THERE. scanners/js_ast.ts emits `ast_attr(M, static,
-- true|false)` for every class method and not one rule had ever read it. Same
-- shape as the flags the census keeps finding: information on the store that
-- nothing consumes cannot go red, so its absence is unobservable.
class_member_static[flow](CD, Key, M) :- member_value[flow](CD, Key, M),
                                         ast_attr[code](M, static, true).
class_member_proto[flow](CD, Key, M)  :- member_value[flow](CD, Key, M),
                                         ast_attr[code](M, static, false).
-- an object literal's members carry no `static` attribute at all, so neither of
-- the two above reaches them and they need their own row rather than a negation
member_plain[flow](O, Key, V) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _),
                                 member_value[flow](O, Key, V).
-- ...AND A MODULE NAMESPACE IS PLAIN IN EXACTLY THIS SENSE, 2026-09-07. It has
-- no `static` attribute on anything and no prototype half, so the two class
-- arms cannot reach it and the object-literal arm is the one it belongs in.
-- Measured, not assumed: without this row `alphaNs.crossed(n)` resolves to
-- nothing even though `member_value` names the export, because all three
-- lookups guard their receiver and a `program` node passes none of the guards.
member_plain[flow](P, Key, V) :- module_object[flow](P, _),
                                 member_value[flow](P, Key, V).

-- WHICH ROLE THE RECEIVER IS IN, syntactically and narrowly: a bare name that
-- IS a class name in this file denotes the CLASS OBJECT; anything else that
-- reaches a class node is an instance of it. DECLARED LIMITATION: `const A =
-- Vat; A.tapped()` reads as an instance and loses the static. Widening it needs
-- the value layer to carry the two roles apart rather than the syntax, which is
-- a bigger change than the defect being fixed.
class_receiver[flow](E) :- class_named[flow](_, Name, File), ident_in[code](E, Name, File).

-- THE THREE LOOKUPS. The negation goes LAST, for the reason spelled out over
-- the prototype chain above: with `O` still unbound it reads as "no receiver
-- anywhere is a class", and the rule silently derives nothing.
may_be_node[flow](N, V2) :- member_node_v[flow](N), ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                            class_receiver[flow](O),
                            may_be_node[flow](O, Obj), selects[flow](N, Key),
                            class_member_static[flow](Obj, Key, V), may_be_node[flow](V, V2).
may_be_node[flow](N, V2) :- member_node_v[flow](N), ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                            may_be_node[flow](O, Obj), selects[flow](N, Key),
                            class_member_proto[flow](Obj, Key, V), may_be_node[flow](V, V2),
                            not class_receiver[flow](O).
may_be_node[flow](N, V2) :- member_node_v[flow](N), ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                            may_be_node[flow](O, Obj), selects[flow](N, Key),
                            member_plain[flow](Obj, Key, V), may_be_node[flow](V, V2).
may_be_lit[flow](N, L)   :- member_node_v[flow](N), ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                            may_be_node[flow](O, Obj), selects[flow](N, Key),
                            member_value[flow](Obj, Key, V), may_be_lit[flow](V, L).

-- A METHOD IS A VALUE. `member_value` names the method node, and a method is
-- not in `node_value_kind` because a method is not an expression — so without
-- this it would be a member with no value and `o.m` would evaluate to nothing.
may_be_node[flow](M, M) :- ast_node[code](M, object_method, _, _).
may_be_node[flow](M, M) :- ast_node[code](M, class_method, _, _).
may_be_node[flow](M, M) :- ast_node[code](M, function_declaration, _, _).

-- ENTRY 6b — A FUNCTION DECLARATION IS REACHED BY ITS NAME. `function f(){}`
-- binds `f` without a declarator, so entry 3 — which reads an initialiser —
-- cannot see it, and every name of a declared function evaluated to nothing.
-- Found by a sequence: `(0, curried)(n)` reaches `curried` and stopped there.
-- DUPLICATION DECLARED: `fn_binding` in rules/js-callgraph.rofl says the same
-- thing for the call graph's own tier 1. Same class as `param_bind`, and it
-- retires the same way — by that tier going through the value question.
may_be_node[flow](E, F) :- ast_node[code](F, function_declaration, File, _),
                           ast_child[code](F, id, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name),
                           ident_in[code](E, Name, File).

-- ENTRY 7 — an object property IS its value.
may_be_node[flow](P, N) :- ast_node[code](P, object_property, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](P, value, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_lit[flow](P, V)  :- ast_node[code](P, object_property, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](P, value, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).

-- ENTRY 8 — `this`, AND WHICH FUNCTION BINDS IT.
--
-- WHAT THIS REPLACED, because the correction is the content: the first version
-- read `anywhere under the method`, and said so — a declared over-approximation
-- with the note that no corpus site exercised it. That note was the defect. An
-- ordinary nested `function () {}` REBINDS `this`; an arrow does not. Under the
-- old premise a `this` inside a nested function or inside an object literal's
-- own method was claimed for the enclosing CLASS, and the corpus contained no
-- such site to say so — the same shape as every other blindness this loop has
-- found by writing the fixture first.
--
-- THE SCOPE QUESTION, EXACTLY: `this` is not a lexical binder, but WHICH
-- construct binds it is decided lexically, and the answer is the nearest
-- enclosing function that is not an arrow. That is why this paragraph is the
-- last cell `w_scope_binding` owned: `this_expression x callgraph` could not be
-- closed while the receiver could be attributed to a function that does not
-- bind it.
edb(this_binds_kind).
this_binds_kind(function_declaration).
this_binds_kind(function_expression).
this_binds_kind(object_method).
this_binds_kind(class_method).
this_binds_kind(class_private_method).
-- NOT arrow_function_expression, and that ABSENCE is the whole rule.

this_binder[flow](F)    :- ast_node[code](F, K, _, _), this_binds_kind(K).
-- ORDERED FOR COST: `this_expression` binds T first (a handful of nodes), then
-- `ast_within` with the CONTAINED node bound hands back that node's ancestors
-- rather than every function's contents.
this_over[flow](F, T)   :- ast_node[code](T, this_expression, _, _),
                           ast_within[code](F, T), this_binder[flow](F).
this_nearer[flow](F, T) :- this_over[flow](F, T), this_over[flow](G, T),
                           ast_within[code](F, G), F != G.
this_host[flow](F, T)   :- this_over[flow](F, T), not this_nearer[flow](F, T).

class_method_of[flow](CD, M) :- obj_like[flow](CD), ast_child[code](CD, body, 0, B),
                                ast_child[code](B, body, _, M),
                                ast_node[code](M, class_method, _, _).
may_be_node[flow](T, CD) :- class_method_of[flow](CD, M), this_host[flow](M, T).

-- ENTRY 9 — AN IMPORTED NAME (w_cg_module_boundary).
--
-- WHAT THE QUEUE ITEM SAID: "a call graph that stops at the file: an imported
-- name resolves to nothing". True, and MEASURED FIRST, because seven notes in a
-- row have been wrong about what was missing. Three things came back:
--
--   * the corpus had exactly TWO import declarations, both `./trace.mjs`, and
--     that module is deliberately NOT scanned — it is the instrument. So the
--     boundary had no site where crossing it was possible at all, and 152 of
--     the 167 unresolved call sites are one imported name, `trace`, used once
--     per instrumented function.
--   * `rules/js-modules.rofl` loaded into the call-graph world derives NOTHING
--     here — `resolved_import` 0, `dangling_import` 0, and `calls_in` and
--     `unresolved_call` byte-identical with and without it. That layer resolves
--     against HOST facts about the disk, which this corpus does not supply.
--   * the scanner already carries every part of the binding: an
--     `import_specifier` with an `imported` and a `local` child, and the
--     declaration's `source` value.
--
-- SO THE ANSWER IS A VALUE FACT, not a call-graph mechanism: an imported name
-- MAY BE the function the other module exports under that name, and the call
-- graph reads it through `may_be_node` like every other receiver.
imports_name[code](Local, Name, Src, File) :-
    ast_node[code](D, import_declaration, File, _),
    ast_child[code](D, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src),
    ast_child[code](D, specifiers, _, Sp),
    ast_child[code](Sp, local, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Local),
    ast_child[code](Sp, imported, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name).

-- ONE ROW PER FILE, from the `program` node. `ast_node[code](_, _, File, _)`
-- would enumerate a hundred thousand facts to learn three strings.
corpus_file[code](File) :- ast_node[code](_, program, File, _).

-- WHERE A SPECIFIER POINTS, whether it is an IMPORT or a RE-EXPORT. Both carry
-- a `source` and neither cares which it is at this step; separating them here
-- would be two copies of one sentence.
module_source[code](N, Src, File) :- ast_node[code](N, import_declaration, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](N, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src).
module_source[code](N, Src, File) :- ast_node[code](N, export_all_declaration, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](N, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src).

-- WHAT SYNTAX CAN DO OF MODULE RESOLUTION, AND NO MORE. A relative specifier
-- with a single path segment names a file by its basename, and the host has
-- already told the model each file's logical name. `./sub/x.mjs` has three
-- segments and is not answered here; a bare specifier is a package and is not
-- a file at all. Both are the modules layer's question and it needs facts about
-- the DISK that no AST can see — `a_no_ast_can_see_the_disk` is already the
-- reason atom for exactly that.
--
-- `str_pre(S, Sep)` IS THE PART BEFORE THE FIRST SEPARATOR, not the first N
-- characters, and the first draft here passed `2` where a separator belongs.
-- The kernel refused it EXACTLY — `hole($rule(...), str_type_error)`, naming
-- the rule — and the rule stayed silently empty, because no world the model is
-- measured in read `hole`. It does now.
module_basename[code](Src, Base) :- module_source[code](_, Src, _),
                                    Head is str_pre(Src, "/"), Head = ".",
                                    N is str_segs(Src, "/"), N = 2,
                                    Base is str_seg(Src, "/", 1).
import_target[code](Src, File) :- module_basename[code](Src, Base),
                                  corpus_file[code](File), File = Base.

-- WHAT A MODULE EXPORTS UNDER A NAME. `export function f()` only — a re-export
-- and a default are different questions and neither is claimed here.
exports_name[code](F, Name, File) :- ast_node[code](E, export_named_declaration, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](E, declaration, 0, F),
                                     fn_kind_v(K), ast_node[code](F, K, _, _),
                                     ast_child[code](F, id, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name).

-- ...AND A RE-EXPORT IS THE SAME QUESTION ASKED OF ANOTHER MODULE. `export *
-- from './x'` makes every NAME x exports an export of this module too — and not
-- x's default, which `export *` deliberately leaves behind. The recursion is
-- positive and is what makes a chain of re-exports work at any depth.
--
-- THE KIND GUARD IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH `module_source`, and this is the price
-- of sharing that relation between the two forms: it holds of an IMPORT too, so
-- without the first literal every `import { x } from './y'` would make the
-- importing file re-export everything y exports. The join is on the same node,
-- so the two literals together say "this source belongs to an export-all".
exports_name[code](F, Name, File) :- ast_node[code](E, export_all_declaration, File, _),
                                     module_source[code](E, Src, File),
                                     import_target[code](Src, Target),
                                     exports_name[code](F, Name, Target).

-- ...AND THE FORM WHOSE EXTERNAL NAME IS NOT ITS INTERNAL ONE
-- (w_export_specifier_forms, 2026-09-08). `export { a as b }` is an
-- ExportNamedDeclaration with NO `declaration` child and a `specifiers` list,
-- so the first arm above — which reads `declaration` — derives NOTHING for it.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE THE RULE, because the item's brief allowed either answer and
-- the difference matters: on a two-file probe with `export { hidden as shown }`
-- beside `export function plain`, `exports_name` returned exactly ONE row,
-- `plain`, and the cross-file call `usesShown -> hidden` was absent from
-- `calls_in` with its site reported as an unresolved `s_identifier`. So the
-- model was SILENT rather than wrong-named. It did not believe the module
-- exported `hidden`; it believed the module exported nothing.
--
-- `may_be_node` ON THE `local` CHILD IS THE WHOLE RESOLUTION, and no new
-- mechanism was needed for the second time in this loop: entry 6 already says
-- an identifier naming a declared function IN THIS FILE evaluates to that
-- function, and the specifier's `local` child is exactly such an identifier.
-- Reading `exported` here instead would name the function `b` denotes, which
-- outside this file is nothing.
--
-- A SPECIFIER UNDER A DECLARATION WITH A `source` IS A DIFFERENT SENTENCE.
-- `export { q } from './d'` re-exports someone ELSE'S binding: its `local`
-- child is a name in the OTHER module and there is no local function for it
-- here. Without this negation a `local` that happened to collide with a
-- function of this file would be re-exported instead — the file-scope defect
-- delta.mjs exists to catch, arriving through a different door.
reexport_decl[code](E) :- ast_node[code](E, export_named_declaration, _, _),
                          ast_child[code](E, source, 0, _).

-- ERASURE, THE SAME TWO MARKERS AN IMPORT CARRIES AND NEITHER IMPLYING THE
-- OTHER: `export type { X }` marks the DECLARATION and leaves the specifier
-- saying "value"; `export { type X }` marks the SPECIFIER. A type-only export
-- names nothing at runtime, so it must not reach `may_be_node`.
export_list_erased[code](E)  :- ast_attr[code](E, export_kind, "type").
export_item_erased[code](Sp) :- ast_attr[code](Sp, export_kind, "type").

export_local[code](L, Ext, File) :-
    ast_node[code](E, export_named_declaration, File, _),
    not reexport_decl[code](E), not export_list_erased[code](E),
    ast_child[code](E, specifiers, _, Sp),
    ast_node[code](Sp, export_specifier, _, _), not export_item_erased[code](Sp),
    ast_child[code](Sp, local, 0, L),
    ast_child[code](Sp, exported, 0, X), ast_name[code](X, Ext).

exports_name[code](F, Ext, File) :- export_local[code](L, Ext, File),
                                    may_be_node[flow](L, F).

-- ...AND THE NAMESPACE RE-EXPORT, which exports a MODULE rather than a name in
-- it. `export * as ns from './m'` is an ExportNamedDeclaration whose one
-- specifier is an ExportNamespaceSpecifier — measured from the scanner, not
-- recalled — with the source on the DECLARATION, an `exported` child on the
-- specifier and NO `local` child anywhere: there is no local name for `* as ns`
-- at all.
--
-- WHAT IT EXPORTS UNDER THAT NAME IS THE OTHER MODULE'S OBJECT, and that node
-- already has a name here: `module_object` is the target file's `program`. So
-- an importer's `ns.f()` is an ordinary member lookup through `member_value`
-- over the exports, exactly as a namespace IMPORT is, and the call graph needs
-- nothing new — the third time in this loop the module boundary has cost a
-- value fact and no mechanism.
module_source[code](N, Src, File) :- ast_node[code](N, export_named_declaration, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](N, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src).

export_ns_name[code](Name, Src, File) :-
    ast_node[code](E, export_named_declaration, File, _),
    ast_child[code](E, specifiers, _, Sp),
    ast_node[code](Sp, export_namespace_specifier, _, _),
    ast_child[code](Sp, exported, 0, X), ast_name[code](X, Name),
    module_source[code](E, Src, File).

exports_name[code](P, Name, File) :- export_ns_name[code](Name, Src, File),
                                     import_target[code](Src, Target),
                                     module_object[flow](P, Target).

may_be_node[flow](E, F) :- imports_name[code](Local, Name, Src, File),
                           import_target[code](Src, Target),
                           exports_name[code](F, Name, Target),
                           ident_in[code](E, Local, File).

-- A NAMESPACE IMPORT BINDS THE MODULE ITSELF, and the module object is a node
-- the store already has: the other file's `program`. Then `alphaNs.crossed(n)`
-- is an ordinary member lookup on it — `member_value` over the exports — and
-- the call graph needs nothing new for the second time in two iterations.
--
-- NEITHER SPECIFIER CARRIES AN `imported` CHILD, which is why `imports_name`
-- above correctly does not fire for them: a namespace binds no single name, and
-- a default's imported name is implicit. Measured from the scanner's output
-- rather than assumed — and the first probe that asked hid the answer behind
-- its own filter, which is the third time in this loop a probe has done that.
imports_ns[code](Local, Src, File) :-
    ast_node[code](D, import_declaration, File, _),
    ast_child[code](D, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src),
    ast_child[code](D, specifiers, _, Sp),
    ast_node[code](Sp, import_namespace_specifier, _, _),
    ast_child[code](Sp, local, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Local).

module_object[flow](P, File) :- ast_node[code](P, program, File, _).

may_be_node[flow](E, P) :- imports_ns[code](Local, Src, File), import_target[code](Src, Target),
                           module_object[flow](P, Target), ident_in[code](E, Local, File).
member_value[flow](P, Name, F) :- module_object[flow](P, Target),
                                  exports_name[code](F, Name, Target).

-- A DEFAULT IMPORT BINDS THE ONE UNNAMED EXPORT. `export default function f()`
-- carries a name in the syntax and it is NOT the name the importer uses, which
-- is the whole reason this is a separate rule rather than a case of the named
-- one: `import adefault from './alpha.mjs'` would work spelled any other way.
imports_default[code](Local, Src, File) :-
    ast_node[code](D, import_declaration, File, _),
    ast_child[code](D, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src),
    ast_child[code](D, specifiers, _, Sp),
    ast_node[code](Sp, import_default_specifier, _, _),
    ast_child[code](Sp, local, 0, L), ast_name[code](L, Local).

exports_default[code](F, File) :- ast_node[code](E, export_default_declaration, File, _),
                                  ast_child[code](E, declaration, 0, F),
                                  fn_kind_v(K), ast_node[code](F, K, _, _).

may_be_node[flow](E, F) :- imports_default[code](Local, Src, File),
                           import_target[code](Src, Target),
                           exports_default[code](F, Target),
                           ident_in[code](E, Local, File).

-- AND THE OTHER HALF IS A FRONTIER WITH A NAME, not a silence: an import whose
-- module this corpus does not contain. `trace` is the one row, and it is one
-- row rather than 152 because the QUESTION is about the module, not about each
-- use of the name.
import_outside_corpus[audit](Src, File) :- module_source[code](_, Src, File),
                                           not import_target[code](Src, _).

-- ENTRY 8b — `this` inside an OBJECT LITERAL'S OWN METHOD is that object.
--
-- The half the old premise could not have: an object method binds `this` to its
-- object, and nothing here said so, so a `this` in an object method nested in a
-- class method answered with the CLASS. Narrowing entry 8 without adding this
-- would have turned a wrong answer into no answer at all.
obj_method_of[flow](O, M) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _),
                             ast_child[code](O, properties, _, M),
                             ast_node[code](M, object_method, _, _).
may_be_node[flow](T, O) :- obj_method_of[flow](O, M), this_host[flow](M, T).

-- ===========================================================================
-- CONSTRUCTION — the rest of w_df_function_forms
--
-- `new C()` and the name `C` both reach the class node, and the model does NOT
-- tell an instance from its class. DECLARED, because the difference is real:
-- a static method belongs to the class and an ordinary method to an instance,
-- so this conflation would accept `inst.staticOne()` and `C.instanceOne()`.
-- It is not fixed here for a reason this repository keeps running into — the
-- corpus contains no site that could show the difference, so a split would be
-- a distinction no gate can see. It is queued as `w_df_instance_vs_class`
-- rather than silently done or silently ignored.
class_named[flow](CD, Name, File) :- obj_like[flow](CD), ast_node[code](CD, _, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](CD, id, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name).

-- ...AND A CLASS EXPRESSION ANSWERS TO ITS BINDING, NOT TO ITS OWN `id`
-- (w_class_expression, 2026-09-08). This relation is not `.name`: every one of
-- its four consumers — the two `may_be_node` arms below, `class_receiver` and
-- `super_of` — asks WHICH NAME REACHES THIS CLASS IN THIS FILE, and for a
-- declaration the `id` is that name only because a declaration's id is also its
-- binding. A class expression separates the two: `const C = class {}` has no
-- `id` at all, and `const W = class Hoist {}` binds `W` on the outside while
-- `Hoist` is in scope only inside the class's own body.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE THE RULE, with the control in the same file, which is the
-- only reason this is a fix rather than a guess: `new Declared().hold()`
-- resolved and `new (class {})().hold()` and `new (class Hoist {})().hold()`
-- resolved to NOTHING, both reported as unresolved `s_member_on_ident`. So the
-- model's opinion about `class_expression` — `obj_like` and `node_value_kind`
-- both name the kind — reached `member_value` and stopped one relation short of
-- anything that could USE it.
--
-- THE `id` ARM IS NOT REPLACED AND MUST NOT BE. A named class expression is
-- reached by BOTH names inside its own file, and `Hoist` is the one V8 reports:
-- measured on this engine, `const W = class Hoist {}` gives `W.name === "Hoist"`
-- and a `new W()` frame reads `Hoist`, while `const C = class {}` gives
-- `C.name === "C"`.
--
-- AND THE `id` ARM OVER-APPROXIMATES FOR THIS FORM, which is measured and left
-- standing rather than hidden. `class_named` is consumed through
-- `ident_in[code]`, which is FILE-scoped, while `sees_binder[code]` above is
-- scoped properly — so `Hoist` written in a function that is not the class's
-- own body reaches the class here and would reach nothing at run time. Probed:
-- a `function leak() { return Hoist; }` beside the class derives
-- `may_be_node(Hoist, <the class>)`. Narrowing it is the same widening
-- `class_receiver` already defers — the scope layer knows the answer and this
-- relation does not ask it — and it is recorded as a finding rather than
-- patched here, because a class expression is the first form where the two
-- disagree at all.
class_named[flow](CD, Name, File) :- ast_node[code](CD, class_expression, File, _),
                                     ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, File, _),
                                     ast_child[code](D, init, 0, CD),
                                     ast_child[code](D, id, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name).

-- the name of a class denotes the class
may_be_node[flow](E, CD) :- class_named[flow](CD, Name, File),
                            ident_in[code](E, Name, File).

-- and so does an expression that constructs one. THE ABSTRACTION IS THE
-- CONFLATION ABOVE: an instance is represented by the class it came from,
-- which is exactly enough to answer `.m` and not enough to refuse a static.
may_be_node[flow](E, CD) :- ast_node[code](E, new_expression, File, _),
                            ast_child[code](E, callee, 0, CE), ast_name[code](CE, Name),
                            class_named[flow](CD, Name, File).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `super` — the receiver that is a DIFFERENT class from the one you are in.
--
-- `class Sub extends Base { m() { return super.m() } }` calls Base's `m`, not
-- its own, and that is the whole content: `this` denotes the class you are in,
-- `super` denotes the one above. One relation and one entry, and the member
-- lookup that already exists does the rest.
--
-- SCOPE-BLIND LIKE ITS NEIGHBOURS: the superclass is found by NAME in the same
-- file, so a class extending an imported one reaches nothing. That is the same
-- boundary `class_named` already draws and it is not widened here.
super_of[flow](CD, SD) :- obj_like[flow](CD), ast_node[code](CD, _, File, _),
                          ast_child[code](CD, super_class, 0, SC), ast_name[code](SC, Name),
                          class_named[flow](SD, Name, File).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHICH CONSTRUCTOR A CLASS ANSWERS WITH, and it is not always its own.
-- `class B extends A {}` has no constructor node at all — the language
-- synthesises one that forwards to A's — so a rule that looked only at B's own
-- body would say the chain ends there.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS WRITTEN, because the two constructions that reach a
-- constructor DO NOT AGREE and no reading of the spec would have told me which
-- shape to give this. With `class A { constructor(){} }`, `class B extends A {}`
-- and `class C extends B { constructor(){ super(); } }`, V8's own CallSite
-- frames report:
--
--     super() inside C   ->   C -> A        the synthesised B frame is SKIPPED
--     new Cask()         ->   Cask -> ...   the synthesised frame IS created,
--                                           and it is the CALLER
--
-- So the walk below is exactly right for `super()` and is deliberately NOT
-- wired into the `new` rule, which stays on the explicit constructor; the
-- caller a synthesised `new` frame should be attributed to is a separate
-- question with no node to point at, recorded as a finding and queued.
--
-- The negation is stratified: `has_own_ctor` reads `own_ctor`, which reads the
-- class body and nothing else, so it does not depend on `ctor_of`.
own_ctor[flow](CD, M)  :- class_method_of[flow](CD, M),
                          ast_attr[code](M, kind, "constructor").
has_own_ctor[flow](CD) :- own_ctor[flow](CD, _).

ctor_of[flow](CD, M)   :- own_ctor[flow](CD, M).
ctor_of[flow](CD, M)   :- super_of[flow](CD, SD), not has_own_ctor[flow](CD),
                          ctor_of[flow](SD, M).

may_be_node[flow](S, SD) :- ast_node[code](S, super, _, _),
                            class_method_of[flow](CD, M), ast_within[code](M, S),
                            super_of[flow](CD, SD).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TWO MORE EXPRESSION FORMS, and the first one is the interesting one.
--
-- A SEQUENCE EVALUATES TO ITS LAST ELEMENT — `(a, b, c)` is `c`. "Last" is a
-- MAXIMUM over the index column, and this kernel has no aggregation, so it is
-- written the way a maximum is written here: as the absence of a later one.
-- The negation is stratified because `seq_later` does not depend on `seq_last`,
-- and the pair is worth reading as the general shape — `max` is `not exists a
-- bigger`, and it costs one extra relation rather than a language feature.
seq_later[flow](E, I) :- ast_node[code](E, sequence_expression, _, _),
                         ast_child[code](E, expressions, I, _),
                         ast_child[code](E, expressions, J, _), I < J.

may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- ast_node[code](E, sequence_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, expressions, I, X),
                           not seq_later[flow](E, I), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- ast_node[code](E, sequence_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, expressions, I, X),
                           not seq_later[flow](E, I), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).

-- A CONDITIONAL MAY BE EITHER BRANCH, and this is the may-set earning its name:
-- `c ? f : g` is f OR g, both rows, and no flow sensitivity is needed to say so
-- because the relation was never claiming to know which. A must-analysis would
-- have to decide; this one does not have to.
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- ast_node[code](E, conditional_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, consequent, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- ast_node[code](E, conditional_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, alternate, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).

-- ...and the same for a literal, which the branch rules above did not have.
-- The asymmetry was invisible while no site needed it: `o[c ? "a" : "b"]()` is
-- a computed key whose two possible values are both known at parse time, and
-- without these two rows it reads as `s_computed_dynamic_key` — the one shape
-- allowed to spend `runtime_dependent`. A may-set that carries nodes and not
-- literals sends a decidable site to the irreducible verdict.
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- ast_node[code](E, conditional_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, consequent, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- ast_node[code](E, conditional_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, alternate, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AWAIT IS TRANSPARENT TO THIS LAYER, and that is a claim about the MAY-SET
-- rather than a claim about promises. `await X` yields whatever the promise X
-- settles to, and this layer has no term for a promise at all — but the two
-- cases it can reach both come out right: `await v` on a plain value is `v`,
-- and `await f()` where `f` is an `async function` reaches f's returns, which
-- is exactly what `may_be_node` already gives a call. The case it is wrong
-- about — a promise CONSTRUCTED and awaited later — reaches the Promise class,
-- and nothing in a corpus this analyser is pointed at declares that, so the
-- over-approximation has nowhere to land. It is the sound direction: a may-set
-- that lost the value would report a smaller call graph and look correct.
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- ast_node[code](E, await_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, argument, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- ast_node[code](E, await_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, argument, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT A GENERATOR YIELDS, which is not what it returns. `returns[flow]` reads
-- `return_statement`; this reads `yield_expression`, and the two are different
-- facts about the same function — `for (const x of gen())` walks the YIELDS and
-- never touches the return value.
--
yields[flow](F, E) :- ast_node[code](Y, yield_expression, _, _), nearest_v[flow](F, Y),
                      ast_child[code](Y, argument, 0, E).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ...AND THE OTHER HALF, 2026-09-05: THE VALUE A GENERATOR RECEIVES.
--
-- `const sent = yield v` evaluates to whatever the CONSUMER passes to
-- `.next(sent)`. Every other edge in this layer runs from a definition
-- outwards; this one arrives from a call site into the middle of a suspended
-- body, which is why it was left out with a note rather than guessed at.
--
-- MEASURED AGAINST V8 BEFORE ANY OF IT WAS WRITTEN:
--   g.next(pickedA)        the yield evaluates to `pickedA`, and calling it works
--   yield* inner()         the sent value passes THROUGH to inner's own yield,
--                          and the delegating expression is inner's RETURN
--   for (x of gen())       never sends anything, so nothing arrives
--
-- WHICH CALL A NAME WAS BOUND TO, and it needs its own relation: `may_be_node`
-- of an identifier bound to a call gives that call's RETURNS, which for a
-- generator is the wrong object entirely — `const g = chooser()` makes `g` the
-- generator, not what `chooser` returns.
bound_to_call[flow](E, C) :- ident_in[code](E, Name, File),
                             binder[code](_, Name, C, File),
                             ast_node[code](C, call_expression, _, _).

-- THE CONSUMER'S SIDE: `g.next(v)` where `g` is bound to a call of generator G.
next_send[flow](G, V) :- call_site[code](C, _), callee_of[code](C, N),
                         -- the KEY IS A STRING, not an atom: `selects` reads
                         -- `ast_name`, and this cost a run to find out
                         selects[flow](N, "next"),
                         ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O), bound_to_call[flow](O, GC),
                         resolves[code](GC, G),
                         ast_child[code](C, arguments, 0, V).

-- DELEGATION PASSES IT THROUGH. `yield* inner()` inside G means a value sent to
-- G's `.next` reaches inner's yields — measured, not assumed.
delegates[flow](Outer, Inner) :- ast_node[code](Y, yield_expression, _, _),
                                 ast_attr[code](Y, delegate, true),
                                 nearest_v[flow](Outer, Y),
                                 ast_child[code](Y, argument, 0, A),
                                 resolves[code](A, Inner).
next_send[flow](Inner, V) :- next_send[flow](Outer, V), delegates[flow](Outer, Inner).

-- ...and what a yield expression may then BE. Only a non-delegating one: the
-- value of `yield* inner()` is not what the consumer sent, it is what inner
-- RETURNED, which is the rule after this.
may_be_node[flow](Y, X) :- next_send[flow](G, V), nearest_v[flow](G, Y),
                           ast_node[code](Y, yield_expression, _, _),
                           ast_attr[code](Y, delegate, false),
                           may_be_node[flow](V, X).

-- THE DELEGATING EXPRESSION IS THE INNER GENERATOR'S RETURN VALUE, which is the
-- one place a generator's `return` is read at all: `for-of` walks the yields and
-- never touches it.
may_be_node[flow](Y, X) :- ast_node[code](Y, yield_expression, _, _),
                           ast_attr[code](Y, delegate, true),
                           ast_child[code](Y, argument, 0, A), resolves[code](A, Inner),
                           returns[flow](Inner, E), may_be_node[flow](E, X).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FOR-OF: the loop variable takes the elements of what is iterated.
for_of_src[flow](S, X)     :- ast_node[code](S, for_of_statement, _, _),
                              ast_child[code](S, right, 0, X).
for_of_name[flow](S, Name) :- ast_node[code](S, for_of_statement, _, _),
                              ast_child[code](S, left, 0, D),
                              ast_child[code](D, declarations, 0, V),
                              ast_child[code](V, id, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name).
for_of_use[flow](S, Name, U) :- for_of_name[flow](S, Name),
                                ast_child[code](S, body, 0, B), ast_within[code](B, U),
                                ident[code](U, Name).

-- WHAT AN ITERABLE HANDS OUT, one element at a time. Two sources and they are
-- reached differently on purpose: an array is a VALUE, so the first rule goes
-- through `may_be_node` and covers both `for (x of [a, b])` and a variable
-- holding that array; a generator is a CALL, so the second reads the call site
-- directly — `may_be_node` of a call gives its RETURNS, and a generator's
-- return value is not what for-of walks.
iter_elem[flow](X, E) :- may_be_node[flow](X, A), ast_node[code](A, array_expression, _, _),
                         ast_child[code](A, elements, _, E).
iter_elem[flow](X, E) :- resolves[code](X, F), yields[flow](F, E).

may_be_node[flow](U, N) :- for_of_use[flow](S, _, U), for_of_src[flow](S, X),
                           iter_elem[flow](X, E), may_be_node[flow](E, N).
may_be_lit[flow](U, V)  :- for_of_use[flow](S, _, U), for_of_src[flow](S, X),
                           iter_elem[flow](X, E), may_be_lit[flow](E, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A LOGICAL OPERATOR IS A CONDITIONAL WITH THE CONDITION SPELT AS AN OPERAND.
-- `a || b` is `a` when a is truthy and `b` otherwise; `a && b` is `a` when a is
-- falsy and `b` otherwise; `a ?? b` is `a` unless a is nullish. In all three
-- BOTH operands are possible values and neither is excluded by anything this
-- layer can see, so the may-set is the union — the same answer as a
-- conditional's two branches, and for the same reason.
--
-- Measured before it was written: `(box || other).get(n)` resolved to nothing,
-- and `(flag ? box : other).get(n)` resolved, on the same corpus, in the same
-- run. The two forms mean the same thing to a reader and one of them was
-- invisible.
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- ast_node[code](E, logical_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, left, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- ast_node[code](E, logical_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, right, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- ast_node[code](E, logical_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, left, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- ast_node[code](E, logical_expression, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](E, right, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AN ASSIGNMENT'S VALUE IS ITS RIGHT-HAND SIDE — and only for a PLAIN one.
-- `(x = o).m()` is `o.m()`; `(x += o).m()` is not, because the value is the sum
-- and this layer knows nothing about sums. The `operator` guard is the whole
-- difference and it is the same lesson the era layer paid for one commit ago:
-- the KIND is not the operation, the operator attribute is.
plain_assign[flow](E) :- ast_node[code](E, assignment_expression, _, _),
                         ast_attr[code](E, operator, "=").

may_be_node[flow](E, N) :- plain_assign[flow](E),
                           ast_child[code](E, right, 0, X), may_be_node[flow](X, N).
may_be_lit[flow](E, V)  :- plain_assign[flow](E),
                           ast_child[code](E, right, 0, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, V).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE CROSSINGS THIS LAYER PERFORMS, DECLARED — and they had never been
-- declared, because no test had ever asked. `leak[audit]` was asserted at zero
-- in two worlds that do not load this file and in a third that does not either;
-- the first world to load rules/js-dataflow.rofl AND look was
-- test/js-controlflow.test.ts on 2026-09-04, and it found five rows that had
-- been standing since the value layer was written.
--
-- All five are real and all five are intended:
--   code  -> flow   every rule here reads the scanner's tree and writes a value
--   main  -> flow   ...and reads the unperspectived tables that classify kinds
--                   (`node_value_kind`, `value_transparent`, `literal_kind`)
--   $kernel -> flow the `edb` declarations above are kernel reflection
imports(flow, code).
imports(flow, main).
imports(flow, $kernel).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE VALUE A `throw` DELIVERS TO A `catch`, 2026-09-06 — queue item
-- w_exception_flow, and it is the first edge in this layer that travels along
-- the EXCEPTION PATH rather than along the syntax. Every other rule here reads
-- a binder, a parameter position or a member key; a catch parameter is bound by
-- something that happens somewhere else in the block.
--
-- THE CORPUS HAD ONE CATCH WITH A PARAMETER AND IT ONLY VOIDED IT, so there was
-- nothing to test against and the fixture came first — the third item running
-- for which that was true.
catch_of[flow](T, H)     :- ast_node[code](T, try_statement, _, _),
                            ast_child[code](T, handler, 0, H).
catch_param[flow](H, P)  :- ast_node[code](H, catch_clause, _, _),
                            ast_child[code](H, param, 0, P).
-- THE GUARDED BLOCK, not the handler and not the finalizer. `try` carries all
-- three under different fields, and a throw inside the HANDLER is not caught by
-- the same clause — reading `ast_within(T, Th)` instead of `ast_within(B, Th)`
-- would have said it was.
try_block[flow](T, B)    :- ast_node[code](T, try_statement, _, _),
                            ast_child[code](T, block, 0, B).
thrown_in[flow](T, V)    :- try_block[flow](T, B), ast_within[code](B, Th),
                            ast_node[code](Th, throw_statement, _, _),
                            ast_child[code](Th, argument, 0, V).

-- A MAY-SET, and it over-approximates in one named way: a throw inside a NESTED
-- try is delivered to the inner handler and this rule offers it to the outer one
-- as well. That is the safe direction for a value layer — an extra candidate,
-- never a missing one — and it is the shape `w_cf_completion` would settle.
caught_value[flow](P, V) :- catch_of[flow](T, H), catch_param[flow](H, P),
                            thrown_in[flow](T, V).
may_be_node[flow](P, V)  :- caught_value[flow](P, V).

-- A CATCH PARAMETER WITH NO LEXICAL THROW IS THREE DIFFERENT THINGS, and the
-- first draft called them all a defect — which put the audit at 1 on an honest
-- checkout, the failure mode this repository has already paid for once. The
-- split was made on 2026-09-06 with TWO classes and gained a third the same day
-- when w_exn_propagation answered the middle one, which is what a frontier with
-- an owner is supposed to do when its owner lands.
call_in_try[flow](T)     :- try_block[flow](T, B), ast_within[code](B, C),
                            call_site[code](C, _).
-- 1. SOURCED THROUGH A CALL EDGE, and ANSWERED rather than deferred since
-- w_exn_propagation: `useTry`'s `catch (e)` receives what `thrower()` throws,
-- and `caught_value` now names the value. The relation stays because the
-- DISTINCTION is still worth reporting — a value that arrives along the call
-- graph is a different kind of answer from one the same block threw.
catch_from_call[flow](P) :- catch_of[flow](T, H), catch_param[flow](H, P),
                            not thrown_in[flow](T, _), call_in_try[flow](T).
-- 2. ...AND THE ONE THE CALL EDGE CANNOT SOURCE. `rethrown`'s block calls only
-- `risky`, which the model says cannot throw — so the only thing that can
-- deliver a value there is the HOST: a TypeError, a RangeError, an allocation
-- failure. That is w_env_api_surface's question and this row is where it is
-- recorded, so the audit below stays exact instead of absorbing it.
catch_from_host[flow](P) :- catch_from_call[flow](P), not caught_value[flow](P, _).

-- ...and THIS is the hole: a handler that binds a name where nothing in the
-- guarded block can throw at all — no `throw`, no call. The parameter has no
-- source under any reading, which is a dead handler rather than a limit of the
-- model, and it is the one of the two worth going red for.
-- 3. AND THIS IS STILL THE HOLE: a handler that binds a name where nothing in
-- the guarded block can throw AND nothing is even called. No source under any
-- reading, host included — a dead handler rather than a limit of the model.
catch_unsourced[audit](P) :- catch_of[flow](T, H), catch_param[flow](H, P),
                             not thrown_in[flow](T, _), not call_in_try[flow](T).
